# A round trip, which is the part only a real database can cover.
#
# The element scripts prove that every pattern is registered and that a script reaches the database
# lookup. This proves the rest: that a value written from a script comes back unchanged, and that
# `last database error` stays quiet when nothing fails. It runs only when the run was pointed at a
# database, so it is the database mode's own check rather than a duplicate of the offline one.
#
# The reads go into global variables on purpose. A global is not touched by the local-variable
# handling the sections do around their asynchronous work, so an empty global means the row really is
# missing rather than lost on the way back. `id` is the key to look at for the same reason: it is
# never null, and a NULL column is invisible in a list variable because Skript deletes a key that is
# set to null.
#
# This is the only script that holds the shared connection across a wait, so the disconnect element
# may not fire while it runs: `server-test/database/elements/16-disconnect.sk` replaces that element
# in database mode and waits for `{skriptorm::selftest::done::roundtrip}`. Without that, the reads below
# report "No database connected." and look exactly like a write that stored nothing.
#
# The NBT column is the other thing only this script can cover. The test server installs SkBee and no
# other NBT implementation, so a compound that goes in through SkBee's syntax and comes back readable
# by SkBee's syntax is what says the plugin works on the servers its users run.
#
# The row is read twice, with a plain Skript `wait` in between. The first read says what the steps
# left behind when they ran as written; the second is a control. If the row appears after the wait,
# then the steps are not ordered the way they are written and the write itself is fine. If it never
# appears, the write put nothing in the table.
#
# Every step reports a failure marker rather than a detail line, because a failure here is a real
# defect and not something the harness should have to infer from a message.
every 5 seconds:
    # A start guard, because every step in this script waits on the database: without it a second
    # firing would begin while the first is still working through the row.
    if {skriptorm::selftest::started::roundtrip} is set:
        stop trigger
    set {skriptorm::selftest::started::roundtrip} to true

    # Nothing is deleted here on purpose: a delete that ran late would remove the very row these
    # checks are about, which is one of the things this script is trying to tell apart.
    #
    # The compound is built with SkBee's own syntax, so what goes in is one of SkBee's objects rather
    # than anything this plugin made: that crossing is what the NBT support exists for.
    insert one entity into table "orm_roundtrip" and wait:
        values:
            name: "Alice"
            age: 30
            data: nbt compound from "{skriptorm:1}"
    if last database error is set:
        log "SKRIPTORM_SELFTEST=FAIL roundtrip insert: %last database error%"

    # The filter is here to give the section a body. Skript warns about any section with nothing indented
    # under its colon, and that warning is Skript's, not this plugin's, with no switch to turn it off. The
    # setup script's own read is the one that covers the form with no `where` at all.
    select one entity from table "orm_roundtrip" and store the result in {orm_selftest::now::*}:
        where all:
            id >= 1
    log "SKRIPTORM_SELFTEST detail: roundtrip now -> %{orm_selftest::now::*}% (error: %last database error%)"

    wait 5 seconds

    select one entity from table "orm_roundtrip" and store the result in {orm_selftest::later::*}:
        where all:
            id >= 1
    log "SKRIPTORM_SELFTEST detail: roundtrip later -> %{orm_selftest::later::*}% (error: %last database error%)"
    if {orm_selftest::later::id} is not set:
        log "SKRIPTORM_SELFTEST=FAIL roundtrip found no row even after waiting"
    if {orm_selftest::later::name} is not "Alice":
        log "SKRIPTORM_SELFTEST=FAIL roundtrip read back name '%{orm_selftest::later::name}%' instead of 'Alice'"
    if {orm_selftest::later::age} is not 30:
        log "SKRIPTORM_SELFTEST=FAIL roundtrip read back age '%{orm_selftest::later::age}%' instead of 30"
    # SkBee renders a compound as SNBT, so its text says whether the tag survived the database and the
    # crossing back into SkBee's type. A column that came back unset renders as '<none>'.
    if "%{orm_selftest::later::data}%" does not contain "skriptorm":
        log "SKRIPTORM_SELFTEST=FAIL roundtrip read back nbt '%{orm_selftest::later::data}%' instead of a compound holding skriptorm"

    # A `where` on the primary key, which does not depend on any stored value being right.
    select one entity from table "orm_roundtrip" and store the result in {_id::*}:
        where all:
            id >= 1
    log "SKRIPTORM_SELFTEST detail: roundtrip by id -> %{_id::*}% (error: %last database error%)"
    if {_id::id} is not set:
        log "SKRIPTORM_SELFTEST=FAIL roundtrip found no row through a where on the primary key"

    log "SKRIPTORM_SELFTEST detail: roundtrip -> ran"
    set {skriptorm::selftest::done::roundtrip} to true
